2.1. By downloading, copying, modifying, distributing, or using any Covered
Materials in circumstances requiring Licensor's permission, You accept this
Agreement. If You do not agree, You must not exercise rights offered only under
this Agreement.
2.2. The restrictions in Sections 3-6 and Schedule A are express conditions of
the license grant, as well as obligations of Licensee to the extent enforceable
under applicable law.
2.3. Nothing in this Agreement limits rights that cannot lawfully be
restricted, including applicable copyright exceptions, fair use or fair
dealing, and the right to refer to facts, ideas, or scientific findings not
protected by copyright. No rights are granted to materials Licensor does not
control.

3. Limited Non-Commercial License Grant

3.1. Subject to continuous compliance with this Agreement, Licensor grants You
a worldwide, non-exclusive, non-transferable, royalty-free copyright license,
for the term of the applicable copyright, solely for Non-Commercial Research
Purposes, to:
(a) access, read, reproduce, and study the Paper;
(b) download, install, run, test, evaluate, reproduce, and modify the Code and
Models;
(c) create Derivative Materials for Non-Commercial Research Purposes; and
(d) reproduce and redistribute the Covered Materials and permitted Derivative
Materials in accordance with Section 5.
3.2. The grant extends only to rights held or licensable by Licensor. No
sublicensing right is granted. Each downstream recipient must rely on its own
permission under this Agreement.
3.3. No commercial license, patent license, trademark license, data license, or
other right is granted by implication, estoppel, or otherwise. Any additional
rights require separate express written authorization from the applicable
rights holder.

4. Strict Prohibition of Commercial Use

4.1. Commercial Use of the Paper, Code, Models, or Derivative Materials is
prohibited without Licensor's prior express written authorization. Attribution,
publication of evaluation results, or making an offering available without
charge does not by itself make a commercial activity permissible.
4.2. You must not use, deploy, incorporate, distribute, sell, license, or
otherwise exploit any Covered Materials or Derivative Materials in or for any
commercial product, service, research program, customer engagement, or business
operation, including internal commercial research and evaluation.
4.3. As a separate undertaking by each Licensee using the Models or Code, You
also agree not to commercially exploit Outputs, including by selling them,
incorporating them into paid or commercial services, or using them to develop
or improve commercially deployed models or systems, unless separately
authorized in writing by Licensor. This contractual obligation does not assert
that Licensor necessarily owns copyright in every Output or bind persons who
have not accepted this Agreement.
4.4. Commercial reproduction, republication, adaptation, or distribution of the
Paper as a protected work is not licensed. This provision does not purport to
prohibit lawful citation, reporting of uncopyrightable facts, independent
implementation of published ideas, or uses permitted by mandatory law.
4.5. Licensor may consider requests for commercial authorization at its sole
discretion. No authorization is effective unless documented in a separate
writing executed by an authorized Licensor representative.

5. Redistribution, Attribution, and Derivative Materials

5.1. For any permitted redistribution of Covered Materials or Derivative
Materials, You must:
(a) retain all applicable copyright, ownership, attribution, and disclaimer
notices;
(b) provide recipients with a complete copy of this Agreement, including
Schedule A, and identify the applicable license version;
(c) conspicuously identify any modifications You made, without implying that
Licensor reviewed or endorsed them;
(d) identify relevant Axion source repositories or publications, when
reasonably practicable; and
(e) ensure Your distribution is limited to Non-Commercial Research Purposes and
does not purport to grant rights inconsistent with this Agreement.
5.2. To the extent Derivative Materials incorporate or are created through
licensed use of Covered Materials or Outputs, You must not distribute them for
Commercial Use, or represent that recipients may commercially use them by
virtue of this Agreement. You may license Your own independent contributions
separately only insofar as doing so does not expand rights in Covered Materials
or relieve You of Your obligations hereunder.
5.3. If You publish research results obtained through use of the Code or
Models, provide reasonable academic attribution to the Axion project and its
applicable paper or repository. Non-commercial scholarly publication of
independently written findings is permitted under this Agreement; any separate
rights required to reproduce the Paper or other Covered Materials in commercial
publications must be obtained from the appropriate rights holders.
5.4. You must not remove, conceal, or misrepresent Licensor's ownership notices
or use ByteDance or Axion names, logos, or trademarks to suggest sponsorship or
endorsement.

6. Mandatory Responsible-Use and Safety Conditions

6.1. You must comply with Schedule A: Acceptable Use and Safety Policy.
Compliance with Schedule A is an express condition of all permissions under
this Agreement.
6.2. You must not knowingly enable, facilitate, or authorize a third party to
engage in conduct that would violate Schedule A if performed by You.
Distribution of materials with the purpose of circumventing these conditions is
prohibited.
6.3. Before making any Derivative Model, tool-enabled agent, or application
available to others, You must conduct reasonable safety testing appropriate to
its capabilities and foreseeable misuse, provide suitable human oversight and
access controls, and clearly disclose significant known limitations relevant to
safe use.
6.4. Legitimate safety evaluation, controlled red-teaming, and defensive
research are permitted only where they are authorized, non-commercial,
conducted with appropriate safeguards, and do not constitute the actual harmful
conduct prohibited in Schedule A.

